2.10.1 Computing Probabilities

In a study, the diameters of 8 widgets are measured. It is known from previous work that the widget diamaters are normally distributed, with sample standard deviation \(S = 1\) unit. What is the probability that the the sample mean \( ar{X}\) observed in the current study will be within one unit of the population mean \(\mu\)?


The probability we seek is \[
P( \vert  ar{X} - \mu \vert &lt; 1 ) = P(\mu - 1 \leq  ar{X} \leq \mu + 1) \,.
\] The key here is that we don’t know \(\sigma\), so the probability can only be determined if we manipulate this expression such that the random variable inside it is \(t\)-distributed…and \( ar{X}\) itself is not. So the first step is to standardize: \[
P\left(  rac{\mu - 1 - \mu}{S/\sqrt{n}} \leq  rac{ ar{X} - \mu}{S/\sqrt{n}} \leq  rac{\mu + 1 - \mu}{S/\sqrt{n}}ight) = P\left( - rac{\sqrt{n}}{S} \leq T \leq  rac{\sqrt{n}}{S}ight) \,.
\] We know that \(\sqrt{n}( ar{X} - \mu)/S\) is \(t\)-distributed (with \(
u = n-1\) degrees of freedom), so long as the individual data are normal iid random variables. (If the individual data are not normally distributed, this question might have to be solved via simulations…if we cannot determine the distribution of \( ar{X}\) using, e.g., moment-generating functions.)


The probability is the difference between two cdf values: \[
P\left( - rac{\sqrt{n}}{S} \leq T \leq  rac{\sqrt{n}}{S}ight) = F_{T(7)}(\sqrt{8}) - F_{T(7)}(-\sqrt{8}) \,,
\] where \(n-1\), the number of degrees of freedom, is 7. This is the end of the problem if we are using pen and paper. If we have R at our disposal, we would code the following:



Code
pt(sqrt(8), 7) - pt(-sqrt(8), 7)


[1] 0.9745364



The probability that \( ar{X}\) will be within one unit of \(\mu\) is 0.975.
